+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Boundary Rendering:** Octree leaves represent uniform blocks, but boundaries between blocks must be rendered carefully. To avoid traversal overhead for boundary values, the original data is kept uncompressed, and the octree serves as a map into this volume data [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Viewing Position:** The optimization assumes a fixed viewing position. Changing the view requires rerendering the entire tree and creating a new compositing tree [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Temporal Navigation:** To allow random temporal navigation, complete trees must be saved at regular intervals [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Back Subtree Approximation:** Due to opacity accumulation obscuring back details, subtrees representing the back portion of the data may not be completely sampled. As an approximation, unchanged subtrees are not re-rendered [S1, S2, unofficial].
+
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The rendering cost is proportional to the amount of *modified* data between time steps, not the total volume size [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. If only a small portion of the volume changes, the cost is significantly lower than full rendering [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the Compositing Tree, which holds partial images for all nodes in the octree [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. This is additional memory overhead compared to standard rendering, but the tree structure is compact [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Significant savings in time domain are achieved because only the difference between consecutive time steps is rendered [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. The time to read encoded data is also reduced in proportion to the compression rate [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:**
-    *   Efficient for time-varying data with localized changes [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-    *   Optimizations for constant subvolumes eliminate interpolation and coordinate calculation overhead [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-    *   Front-to-back rendering and transparent space skipping reduce unnecessary computation [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:**
-    *   **Fixed Viewing Position:** The optimization assumes a fixed viewing position [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. Changing the view requires rerendering the entire tree and creating a new compositing tree [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-    *   **Temporal Navigation:** To allow random navigation through time, complete trees must be saved at regular intervals, increasing storage requirements [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-    *   **Approximation:** Due to opacity accumulation obscuring back details, unchanged subtrees in the back may not be completely sampled [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. This is an approximation that may introduce visual artifacts if the front changes significantly [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-    *   **Boundary Overhead:** Handling boundaries between uniform blocks requires access to uncompressed data, adding complexity [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Proportional to the amount of *modified* data, not the total volume. Savings are significant when changes between time steps are small [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the Compositing Tree, which holds partial images for all nodes. This is roughly equivalent to storing the full image for the entire volume at each time step if no changes occur, but allows reuse of static parts.
+*   **Performance:** Rendering from the tree structure incurs overhead compared to direct volume data rendering. This is offset by optimizations like front-to-back rendering (early ray termination), caching ray coordinates in object space, and skipping transparent space [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Efficient for time-varying data with localized changes; reduces I/O by leveraging compression [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Inefficient for global view changes; requires significant memory to store partial images for the compositing tree; approximation errors may occur for obscured back regions [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Compositing Tree Rendering was introduced in the context of efficient encoding and rendering of time-varying volume data [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. It was developed to address the high computational cost of rendering large, time-varying datasets by leveraging data compression and hierarchical structures [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. The method was tested on datasets such as Turbulent Vortex Flow, Thermal Convection, and Heart Modeling, with spatial resolutions up to $128^3$ [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
+Compositing Tree Rendering was introduced in the context of efficient encoding and rendering of time-varying volume data. It leverages octree-based compression to identify modified regions and uses a compositing tree to incrementally update the rendered image. The method was implemented in the `tvvd-renderer` and tested on datasets such as Turbulent Vortex Flow, Thermal Convection, and Heart Modeling [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
